From: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
Subject: [PATCH] scsi: ufs: qcom: drive the lane clocks only from suspend/resume

The downstream sdm845 driver touches the lane clocks from exactly four places:
enable in ufs_qcom_hce_enable_notify() and ufs_qcom_resume(), disable in
ufs_qcom_suspend() and ufs_qcom_exit(). Its ufs_qcom_setup_clocks() does not
touch them at all.

Mainline adds a second enable/disable pair inside ufs_qcom_setup_clocks(),
both conditional on ufs_qcom_is_link_hibern8(). That gives two enables per
runtime resume against one effective disable per suspend -- the disable in
ufs_qcom_setup_clocks() is swallowed by the is_lane_clks_enabled guard that
ufs_qcom_suspend() has already cleared. Every cycle therefore leaks one
reference on each lane clock, gcc_ufs_phy_* never reach zero, gpll0 and the
rpmhcc XO stay up, xo.lvl is pinned in the RPMH sleep set and aosd/cxsd stay 0.

It also makes the asymmetry unfixable in place. Guarding the enable (which is
what downstream does) hangs the controller here, because the setup_clocks
enable is conditional: when the link is not hibern8 on resume that path does
not enable, and a guarded ufs_qcom_resume() then skips its enable too on a
stale is_lane_clks_enabled, leaving the lane clocks off. The host wedges at
power/runtime_status "resuming", SCSI never calls blk_post_runtime_resume(),
and every LUN queue stays frozen -- silently, with no UIC error.

Drop the mainline-only pair so the call sites match downstream. resume() then
enables once and suspend() disables once, the count returns to zero while
suspended, and no path can skip an enable that a resume needs.

Signed-off-by: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
---
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
@@ -1466,13 +1466,13 @@
 	case PRE_CHANGE:
 		if (on) {
 			ufs_qcom_icc_update_bw(host);
-			if (ufs_qcom_is_link_hibern8(hba)) {
-				err = ufs_qcom_enable_lane_clks(host);
-				if (err) {
-					dev_err(hba->dev, "enable lane clks failed, ret=%d\n", err);
-					return err;
-				}
-			}
+			/*
+			 * No lane-clk enable here. The downstream driver drives
+			 * the lane clocks only from ufs_qcom_resume() and
+			 * ufs_qcom_suspend(); this extra pair is mainline-only
+			 * and made two enables race one effective disable per
+			 * cycle, leaking a reference and pinning the XO.
+			 */
 		} else {
 			if (!ufs_qcom_is_link_active(hba)) {
 				/* disable device ref_clk */
@@ -1498,8 +1498,7 @@
 			if (ufshcd_is_hs_mode(&hba->pwr_info))
 				ufs_qcom_dev_ref_clk_ctrl(host, true);
 		} else {
-			if (ufs_qcom_is_link_hibern8(hba))
-				ufs_qcom_disable_lane_clks(host);
+			/* Matching the above: the disable belongs to suspend. */
 
 			ufs_qcom_icc_set_bw(host, ufs_qcom_bw_table[MODE_MIN][0][0].mem_bw,
 					    ufs_qcom_bw_table[MODE_MIN][0][0].cfg_bw);
